WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.894
The wave, heat and Laplace equations have very different solutions.

2
00:00:05.894 --> 00:00:06.245
Why?

3
00:00:06.245 --> 00:00:11.699
The answer lies in the coefficients of the second derivatives.

4
00:00:11.699 --> 00:00:15.921
In this part we learn how to write the equation,

5
00:00:15.921 --> 00:00:24.014
compute the discriminant and tell apart the three types: hyperbolic, parabolic and elliptic.

6
00:00:24.264 --> 00:00:32.569
Important convention: we write the equation as a u x x plus two b u x y plus c u y y.

7
00:00:32.569 --> 00:00:35.403
The mixed term carries two b.

8
00:00:35.403 --> 00:00:39.311
The discriminant is b squared minus a c.

9
00:00:39.311 --> 00:00:44.881
Some books drop the two and use b squared minus four a c.

10
00:00:44.881 --> 00:00:49.766
The sign is the same, but never mix the notations!

11
00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:57.286
If the discriminant is positive the equation is hyperbolic, like the wave equation.

12
00:00:57.286 --> 00:01:02.016
If it is zero it is parabolic, like the heat equation.

13
00:01:02.016 --> 00:01:07.097
If it is negative it is elliptic, like Laplace’s equation.

14
00:01:07.097 --> 00:01:12.791
It mirrors the second-order curves: hyperbola, parabola, ellipse.

15
00:01:12.791 --> 00:01:16.382
Lower-order terms do not affect the type.

16
00:01:16.632 --> 00:01:22.203
Example: u x x plus two u x y plus two u y y equals zero.

17
00:01:22.203 --> 00:01:29.240
The mixed coefficient is two, so two b equals two, that is b equals one.

18
00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.270
And a equals one, c equals two.

19
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:37.145
The discriminant is one minus two, which is minus one.

20
00:01:37.145 --> 00:01:40.399
Negative, so the equation is elliptic.

21
00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:47.080
If you wrongly take b equal to two, you get four minus two, a positive number,

22
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.992
and the wrong answer “hyperbolic”.

23
00:01:49.992 --> 00:01:53.846
So always take half of the mixed coefficient.

24
00:01:54.096 --> 00:01:58.981
With variable coefficients the type depends on the region.

25
00:01:58.981 --> 00:02:03.950
The Tricomi equation: y times u x x plus u y y equals zero.

26
00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.309
The discriminant is minus y.

27
00:02:06.309 --> 00:02:14.310
The graph shows the line y equals zero: above it the equation is elliptic, below it hyperbolic,

28
00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.500
and on the line parabolic.

29
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.638
It marks the border between subsonic and supersonic gas flow.

30
00:02:21.888 --> 00:02:29.067
Check yourself: what is b in the equation on screen, and what type is it?

31
00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:33.590
Remember to halve the mixed coefficient first.
